WEBVTT

00:01.724 --> 00:04.804
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.872 --> 00:08.221
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.288 --> 00:11.369
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.437 --> 00:14.350
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.419 --> 00:18.302
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.575 --> 00:21.437
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.522 --> 00:23.062
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.600 --> 00:24.940
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:25.007 --> 00:26.982
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.458 --> 00:36.897
Okay, okay, I got one.

00:36.964 --> 00:40.549
Would you rather have dinner and
then sex with Megan Fox's body

00:40.616 --> 00:42.255
with Leonard Nimoy's
head on it

00:42.324 --> 00:44.902
or reroof an entire
condo complex by yourself?

00:44.970 --> 00:46.141
Oh, boy.

00:46.210 --> 00:47.515
Can I close my eyes?

00:47.584 --> 00:50.161
No, eye contact the whole time
and a lot of kissing.

00:50.230 --> 00:53.142
Well, how about Megan Fox's head
on Leonard Nimoy's body?

00:53.210 --> 00:54.817
I know, somehow
that's better.

00:54.886 --> 00:57.228
The hybrid organism exists
as I've described it.

00:57.296 --> 00:58.635
How many units
are in the condo?

00:58.703 --> 00:59.641
Thirty-two.

00:59.708 --> 01:00.846
Tile roof or shingles?

01:00.915 --> 01:01.985
Yeah, 'cause it
makes a difference

01:02.053 --> 01:03.124
what you're carrying
up that ladder.

01:03.192 --> 01:04.565
Wait, whose neck is it?
Huh?

01:04.632 --> 01:05.871
I mean, where's the cutoff?

01:05.939 --> 01:07.545
You know,
is it Megan Fox's neck

01:07.613 --> 01:09.856
or am I burying my face in
Leonard Nimoy's turkey gizzard?

01:09.924 --> 01:11.096
Nimoy's neck.

01:11.164 --> 01:12.837
You have got yourself a roof.

01:12.905 --> 01:14.579
I'll take the
Spock-Fox intercourse.

01:14.647 --> 01:15.852
All right, Peter,
your turn.

01:15.920 --> 01:19.067
Uh... boxers or briefs?

01:19.136 --> 01:20.307
What?

01:20.375 --> 01:22.015
That's the worst one
I've ever heard.

01:22.083 --> 01:23.020
Look, I'm sorry.

01:23.088 --> 01:24.326
I'm exhausted, all right?

01:24.395 --> 01:25.767
I-I didn't get any sleep
'cause Brian

01:25.834 --> 01:27.810
and the neighbor's dog
kept me up all night.

01:27.878 --> 01:29.920
BRIAN:
Hello?

01:29.989 --> 01:31.594
Hello?
DOG: Hello.

01:31.662 --> 01:33.303
Are you a dog?
Yes!

01:33.371 --> 01:34.576
I am also a dog.

01:34.644 --> 01:35.916
All right!
Yeah!

01:35.983 --> 01:37.122
We're dogs!
Yeah, we're dogs!

01:37.190 --> 01:38.730
We're dogs that live
near each other!

01:38.797 --> 01:40.169
DOG 2: Hey, are
you guys dogs?

01:40.237 --> 01:41.442
Yeah!
We're totally dogs!

01:41.510 --> 01:42.615
Yeah, both of us!

01:42.683 --> 01:44.256
Oh, wow. Well, if you're
that tired, Peter,

01:44.325 --> 01:45.597
maybe you should
have some coffee.

01:45.664 --> 01:47.303
Yeah, or you could
have some Red Bull.

01:47.372 --> 01:48.912
Red Bull?
What the hell is Red Bull?

01:48.980 --> 01:50.152
You never heard of Red Bull?

01:50.219 --> 01:51.290
It's an energy drink.

01:51.359 --> 01:53.735
Here you go, Peter.
It's on the house.

01:57.823 --> 02:01.206
So? What do you think?

02:01.273 --> 02:04.437
♪ And I feel
like I just got home ♪

02:04.522 --> 02:06.363
♪ And I feel ♪

02:09.078 --> 02:12.760
♪ And I feel
like I just got home ♪

02:12.829 --> 02:17.048
♪ And I feel... ♪

02:22.341 --> 02:23.379
Good evening.

02:23.447 --> 02:24.886
Our top story tonight:

02:24.954 --> 02:27.632
Barack Obama makes a visit
to James Woods High School.

02:27.701 --> 02:29.508
Yes, it's time to get
jiggy with it, Quahog--

02:29.576 --> 02:30.982
I thought
we had taken that out.

02:31.050 --> 02:33.494
President Barack Obama
will visit James Woods High

02:33.562 --> 02:36.642
next month, and this little town
of ours couldn't be more abuzz.

02:36.710 --> 02:39.390
Oh, my God, this is
gonna be so amazing!

02:39.458 --> 02:41.969
It's all anybody in my class
is talking about.

02:42.037 --> 02:44.280
Darn it, why is
this so hard?!

02:44.348 --> 02:45.954
What's wrong, Chris?

02:46.023 --> 02:48.735
Just 'cause Obama's coming,
everybody in my English class

02:48.803 --> 02:51.214
has to write a stupid
essay about hope.

02:51.282 --> 02:53.458
But this is all I
can come up with.

02:53.525 --> 02:56.439
Chris, this is just a shaded-in
triangle and the word "muff."

02:56.507 --> 02:58.315
For God's sakes, Meg,
it's a first draft.

02:58.382 --> 03:00.257
Just tell me if you
think the tone is right.

03:00.326 --> 03:01.631
Well, I don't know
what this is,

03:01.699 --> 03:03.573
but it's not what hope
is about, Chris.

03:03.642 --> 03:06.755
Well, if you're such a genius,
you tell me what hope is.

03:06.824 --> 03:10.473
Hope is what gets you
out of bed in the morning

03:10.541 --> 03:13.555
when it's the day of prom
and you haven't been asked.

03:13.623 --> 03:16.000
Hope pushes the caterpillar
through the cocoon

03:16.068 --> 03:18.580
and drives the salmon upstream.

03:18.647 --> 03:22.096
Your breasts may be small
and your glasses may be thick,

03:22.164 --> 03:23.771
but hope doesn't
hold up a mirror.

03:23.839 --> 03:25.915
Hope is a horizon we head for,

03:25.983 --> 03:28.461
leaving nothing behind us
but fear.

03:28.528 --> 03:30.804
And though we may
never reach our goals,

03:30.873 --> 03:34.222
it's hope that will save us
from who we once were.

03:34.290 --> 03:35.695
Wow.

03:35.764 --> 03:37.236
Did you learn
that at school?

03:37.304 --> 03:40.051
No. You can't just learn
creative writing, Chris.

03:40.118 --> 03:43.767
It's got to be inside you,
like musical talent

03:43.835 --> 03:47.352
or athleticism or the ability
to choose to be gay.

03:47.419 --> 03:48.959
I don't think
they choose that.

03:49.027 --> 03:51.807
Oh, please,
they choose that.

03:51.875 --> 03:54.387
(doorknob rattling)

03:54.454 --> 03:56.530
Hey, hey, hey,
hey, hey, hey, hey!

03:56.598 --> 03:58.070
What's up, party people?
Ah, what the hell

03:58.138 --> 03:59.945
are these things,
curtains or something? Boring!

04:00.014 --> 04:01.654
- Holy smoke, it's crowded in here.
- Hey!

04:01.722 --> 04:03.731
There, ah, that's terrific;
it opens up the whole room.

04:03.799 --> 04:04.804
Peter, there
you are.

04:04.855 --> 04:05.808
Where the hell
have you been?

04:05.875 --> 04:07.080
I've been
worried sick.

04:07.148 --> 04:08.889
Oh, so I had a few Red Bulls,
drove to New York.

04:08.957 --> 04:10.413
What's the big deal?
Ah, there's my little man!

04:10.498 --> 04:12.557
(shuddering)

04:19.509 --> 04:20.579
Done!

04:20.647 --> 04:22.856
Wow, Dad, that
was pretty cool.

04:22.925 --> 04:23.962
Can I try
some Red Bull?

04:24.030 --> 04:25.235
Oh, my God, can you
try some Red Bull?

04:25.303 --> 04:26.675
Chris, I'd take it
as an insult if you didn't.

04:26.743 --> 04:28.317
Here you go, have some cans,
one, two, three, four, five.

04:28.385 --> 04:30.092
Go on, take 'em, take 'em,
take 'em, take 'em, take 'em.

04:30.143 --> 04:31.399
(laughs) That's what
Woody Woodpecker says.

04:31.466 --> 04:32.872
All right, I'm gonna go outside
and milk the cow.

04:34.648 --> 04:37.193
Peter, where'd
you get that cow?

04:37.261 --> 04:38.601
Oh, that's a silly
question, Lois.

04:38.668 --> 04:40.610
I bought it so we can have
fresh milk every day.

04:43.726 --> 04:45.399
(mooing)

04:45.467 --> 04:47.711
Whoa, boy, that Red Bull's
some strong stuff.

04:47.779 --> 04:49.318
(screaming)

04:56.219 --> 04:57.558
<i>Hey, everybody, welcome back
to The Price Is Right.</i>

04:57.609 --> 04:58.630
Time to spin the wheel.

04:58.699 --> 04:59.768
Top winner
for the day is Peter,

04:59.837 --> 05:01.276
so you're gonna be
the first to spin it.

05:01.328 --> 05:02.515
Go ahead, Peter,
close as you can

05:02.584 --> 05:04.727
to a dollar
without going over.

05:04.794 --> 05:06.635
(beeping)

05:08.964 --> 05:10.303
All right, while we're waiting
for the wheel to spin,

05:10.372 --> 05:11.525
you want to say hi to anybody?

05:11.594 --> 05:12.932
Oh, yeah, Drew, I
want to say hi to

05:13.001 --> 05:14.608
Lois, Brian, Chris, Stewie,
Meg, Joe, Bonnie, Quagmire,

05:14.675 --> 05:16.517
Cleveland, Mort, Seamus, Adam
West, Dr. Hartman, Bruce,

05:16.584 --> 05:18.560
Carter, Babs, Tom Tucker,
Angela, Opie, Carl, Herbert,

05:18.628 --> 05:20.703
Jillian, Consuela, Giant
Chicken, Greased-up Deaf Guy!

05:20.772 --> 05:21.909
(chuckles):
Okay.

05:21.977 --> 05:24.086
I'm sure they're
happy to hear that.

05:25.997 --> 05:27.503
(crowd screaming)

05:29.782 --> 05:31.656
Whoa, paramedics,
come on down!

05:31.724 --> 05:33.565
(laughing frantically)

05:41.538 --> 05:42.945
Mom, oh, my God,
guess what!

05:43.013 --> 05:45.122
Oh, God! You scared
the (bleep) out of me.

05:45.189 --> 05:47.601
You know that essay about hope
I had to write?

05:47.669 --> 05:50.213
Well, the principal said it was
the best one in the class,

05:50.281 --> 05:53.093
and he wants me to read it aloud
to introduce the President!

05:53.161 --> 05:55.439
Ah, Chris,
that's incredible!

05:55.506 --> 05:57.883
Remember when I used to
come to school to wipe you,

05:57.952 --> 06:00.496
and I'd say, "Someday I won't
have to wipe you anymore

06:00.565 --> 06:02.338
and you'll do great things"?

06:02.407 --> 06:04.750
Well, this has been
a big year for us.

06:04.817 --> 06:07.345
Wait a second.
Give me that.

06:07.413 --> 06:09.389
"Hope pushes the caterpillar
through the cocoon

06:09.457 --> 06:11.533
and drives the salmon upstream."

06:11.601 --> 06:13.107
I wrote that!

06:13.175 --> 06:14.581
You stole
this from me.

06:14.648 --> 06:16.556
Hey, you didn't say
I couldn't use it.

06:16.625 --> 06:19.270
But now you get to meet the
President of the United States

06:19.339 --> 06:20.778
because of
what I wrote.

06:20.845 --> 06:23.323
Ugh! I bet you don't even
know what his first name is.

06:23.391 --> 06:25.701
I-I don't know-- Mike?

06:27.695 --> 06:29.236
Hey, what are
you doing?

06:29.303 --> 06:32.652
Lois, where is my Red Bull?

06:32.720 --> 06:34.125
Peter, I got
rid of it.

06:34.193 --> 06:35.700
Why the hell
would you do that?!

06:35.768 --> 06:38.413
It was making you crazy,
and it's not good for you.

06:38.481 --> 06:40.623
That's why I poured it
all out in the garden.

06:44.779 --> 06:47.457
(grunting)

06:49.300 --> 06:50.220
(tires screech)

06:50.288 --> 06:53.252
Official flower business.

06:53.319 --> 06:54.959
(tires screeching)

06:57.640 --> 06:58.945
What are you doing, Peter?

06:59.014 --> 07:00.453
I'm making my
own Red Bull.

07:00.521 --> 07:02.462
Lois can't stop me
from experiencing

07:02.530 --> 07:05.075
the manic highs and
lows my body demands.

07:05.143 --> 07:07.018
Whoa, whoa, hang on,
you're adding kerosene?

07:07.086 --> 07:08.291
Peter, that's insane.

07:08.359 --> 07:09.597
That'll destroy your body.

07:09.665 --> 07:11.071
Kerosene is
fuel, Brian.

07:11.138 --> 07:12.444
Red Bull is fuel.

07:12.511 --> 07:13.583
Kerosene is
Red Bull.

07:13.651 --> 07:14.823
Now, why don't you
leave me alone

07:14.890 --> 07:16.296
while I'm doing
my important work?

07:16.364 --> 07:17.502
(blender whirring)

07:17.570 --> 07:19.244
Peter, that drink will kill you.

07:19.312 --> 07:22.459
Brian, whatever kills
me makes me stronger.

07:22.528 --> 07:23.564
(sighs)

07:23.632 --> 07:25.675
See, Brian?
I feel great.

07:25.743 --> 07:27.316
Peter?

07:27.384 --> 07:29.259
Peter, are you alive?

07:33.346 --> 07:34.250
(groaning)

07:34.318 --> 07:35.254
Ah, you're alive.

07:35.322 --> 07:37.163
Okay, I won't, uh,

07:37.231 --> 07:39.978
I won't, uh, eat you then.

07:40.046 --> 07:41.551
I was gonna eat you.

07:45.975 --> 07:47.347
Dr. Hartman,

07:47.414 --> 07:50.059
please tell me, is my
husband gonna be all right?

07:50.127 --> 07:51.466
Uh, Cancun, actually,
thanks for asking.

07:51.534 --> 07:52.739
Just got back.

07:52.806 --> 07:54.917
Mrs. Griffin, I'm afraid
all the toxic chemicals

07:54.984 --> 07:57.228
your husband ingested from
his homemade energy drink

07:57.296 --> 07:58.601
have caused
total kidney failure.

07:58.669 --> 08:01.415
(all gasping)

08:01.482 --> 08:03.189
How was Cancun?

08:07.614 --> 08:09.689
(gasps)
Oh, my God.

08:09.751 --> 08:11.157
Kidney failure?

08:11.226 --> 08:12.564
Is he gonna be okay?

08:12.632 --> 08:13.937
Well, yes and no.

08:14.005 --> 08:15.678
We'll put him on
a kidney transplant list,

08:15.747 --> 08:17.018
but that could take months.

08:17.087 --> 08:19.196
Until a donor is found,
he's gonna have to undergo

08:19.264 --> 08:20.904
dialysis treatments
three times a week.

08:20.972 --> 08:22.244
Dialysis?

08:22.312 --> 08:23.919
Is there any other way?

08:23.986 --> 08:25.627
Yes, there is.

08:25.695 --> 08:26.867
No, no, no, no, no!

08:26.935 --> 08:28.608
W-We'll do
the dialysis.

08:33.935 --> 08:35.274
Hey, Doc, I'm here
for my dialysis.

08:35.343 --> 08:36.412
Ah, have a seat, Peter.

08:36.480 --> 08:38.255
Let's get you hooked up.

08:38.322 --> 08:40.265
Doc, I don't know how much
more of this I can take.

08:40.332 --> 08:41.136
It's been
three weeks.

08:41.204 --> 08:42.659
I know, I saw the caption.

08:42.745 --> 08:44.819
Yeah, but I-I just go crazy
just sitting here for so long.

08:44.888 --> 08:46.227
Aw, I know it's tough,

08:46.296 --> 08:48.136
but maybe there's something
I can do to make it easier.

08:48.204 --> 08:50.347
Here, play with this
bucket of afterbirth.

08:54.234 --> 08:55.472
Ha!

08:55.540 --> 08:56.745
The Lockhorns.

08:56.813 --> 08:58.620
Always locking horns.

08:58.688 --> 09:01.165
(gasps) Oh, my God,
that's why they call it that.

09:05.923 --> 09:07.730
Oh, Peter, you got here
just in time!

09:07.799 --> 09:08.836
Chris is almost on.

09:08.904 --> 09:10.712
Shh. Mom, here it is.

09:10.780 --> 09:12.587
As Quahog prepares
for the arrival of

09:12.656 --> 09:15.200
President Barack Obama,
it will be a very big day

09:15.269 --> 09:17.746
for one local teenager
who will be reading his essay

09:17.814 --> 09:19.320
to introduce the President.

09:19.388 --> 09:22.202
I sat down with accomplished
wordsmith Chris Griffin.

09:22.269 --> 09:24.412
"Hope is a horizon
we head for,

09:24.480 --> 09:26.690
leaving nothing
behind us but fear."

09:26.758 --> 09:28.800
Chris, tell me
how those words came to you.

09:28.868 --> 09:30.642
Do you believe
in angels, Tom?

09:30.710 --> 09:31.814
Of course I do.

09:31.883 --> 09:32.819
Well, there you go.

09:32.887 --> 09:33.925
I suspected angels.

09:33.993 --> 09:35.130
It was angels.
Very good.

09:35.199 --> 09:37.006
Chris Griffin,
a weaver of dreams

09:37.074 --> 09:39.585
at the loom of the mind.

09:39.654 --> 09:43.638
A weaver of dreams at
the loom of the mind.

09:43.706 --> 09:45.179
A neat thing I just said.

09:45.247 --> 09:46.552
And now sports.

09:46.620 --> 09:49.433
Oh, Chris, we are
so proud of you.

09:49.502 --> 09:51.543
I got to admit, Chris,
this is pretty exciting.

09:51.611 --> 09:53.888
When did you become so coherent?

09:53.956 --> 09:55.830
Mom, I wrote that!

09:55.899 --> 09:58.041
He stole it, and he's
taking all the credit!

09:58.109 --> 09:59.582
Chris, is that true?

09:59.650 --> 10:00.955
You didn't write anything!

10:01.023 --> 10:02.429
This is my essay!

10:02.497 --> 10:03.970
Meg, is that true?

10:04.038 --> 10:05.745
No, he's lying!

10:05.813 --> 10:08.056
That should be me getting
to meet the President!

10:08.123 --> 10:09.664
Meg, for God's sake, relax.

10:09.732 --> 10:12.042
You're not the first person
to be outshined by a sibling.

10:12.110 --> 10:13.884
What about the third
Bronte sister?

10:13.952 --> 10:17.435
<i>Oh, Emily, Wuthering Heights
was truly splendid.</i>

10:17.503 --> 10:20.985
<i>Oh, no, Charlotte, Jane Eyre
was so very brilliant.</i>

10:21.053 --> 10:23.195
I made blood out
me lady parts!

10:23.264 --> 10:24.268
Good for you.

10:24.336 --> 10:25.574
So we've all
done something.

10:25.642 --> 10:27.483
It's happening now.

10:27.551 --> 10:29.159
Ah, it's a
period joke.

10:29.226 --> 10:30.665
It's a period period joke.

10:30.734 --> 10:31.671
Ha, ha.

10:31.739 --> 10:32.742
Okay, moving on.

10:36.294 --> 10:38.537
<i>ANNOUNCER (over TV): Coming up
next, Charles in Charge.</i>

10:38.605 --> 10:40.010
Oh, my God!

10:40.078 --> 10:43.327
Aw, I want to watch this, but
I got stupid dialysis at 3:00.

10:43.395 --> 10:45.939
I am so sick of this crap
mucking up my life.

10:46.008 --> 10:48.050
Well, Peter, I mean, what,
y-you can get away with

10:48.118 --> 10:49.222
skipping one treatment, right?

10:49.290 --> 10:50.797
I mean, that's not
gonna kill you.

10:50.864 --> 10:52.639
Yeah, it's probably just
like missing an antibiotic.

10:52.706 --> 10:53.845
You just take
the next one.

10:53.913 --> 10:54.949
Yeah, you're probably right.

10:55.018 --> 10:56.558
<i>Besides,
it's Charles in Charge.</i>

10:56.625 --> 10:57.931
JOE:
You know what I like?

10:57.998 --> 10:59.338
The "Charles"
part is formal,

10:59.406 --> 11:02.587
but the "In Charge"
part is kind of casual.

11:02.654 --> 11:04.177
QUAGMIRE:
Oh, wow. Remember this?

11:04.246 --> 11:06.740
Remember when nobody had
any muscle tone?

11:06.809 --> 11:09.151
Geez, how many "A's"
does that guy need?

11:09.219 --> 11:10.927
Uh, two, apparently.

11:10.995 --> 11:12.904
You know, it's kinda
weird that '80s white clothes

11:12.971 --> 11:14.879
could pass for
'90s black clothes.

11:14.931 --> 11:17.425
Yeah, is that like
a rummage sale thing?

11:17.493 --> 11:19.468
Ah, Nicole Eggert.

11:19.536 --> 11:22.048
Didn't she bang
somebody gross?

11:22.115 --> 11:23.487
I don't know.

11:23.555 --> 11:25.196
Yeah, I feel like she
banged somebody gross.

11:25.263 --> 11:26.569
You know, I heard
the sweater budget

11:26.621 --> 11:29.082
on this show
was 200 grand.

11:29.133 --> 11:30.221
Didn't you tell me
that, Peter?

11:31.662 --> 11:33.202
Oh, my God!

11:33.269 --> 11:34.575
Peter, you don't
look so good.

11:34.642 --> 11:36.685
What are you talkin' about?
I feel great.

11:36.754 --> 11:38.763
Like I could go
another 20 years or more.

11:44.658 --> 11:47.403
Dr. Hartman, this was
just too close a call.

11:47.472 --> 11:49.614
Next time, we could be too late.

11:49.682 --> 11:52.159
Suppose he's stuck somewhere,
or-or he's trapped in

11:52.228 --> 11:54.236
an elevator
or God knows what else?!

11:54.305 --> 11:56.582
He could die!
He needs a new kidney now!

11:56.649 --> 11:58.960
Well, as I said, Mrs.
Griffin, he's on a list,

11:59.027 --> 12:01.137
but there are a lot
of people ahead of him.

12:01.204 --> 12:03.984
Well, what are the chances of
getting him moved up the list?

12:04.052 --> 12:06.027
Honestly, not great.
It could be months

12:06.096 --> 12:07.468
before he can get
a transplant.

12:07.536 --> 12:09.477
Unless you know someone
who's a compatible organ match

12:09.545 --> 12:11.420
and would be willing
to donate one of theirs.

12:11.488 --> 12:13.966
I'll do it.
He could have one of my kidneys.

12:14.034 --> 12:16.343
Well, we always check spouse
records for compatibility.

12:16.412 --> 12:17.717
I'm afraid you're not a match.

12:17.785 --> 12:20.363
But it turns out you are a match
for a little girl

12:20.432 --> 12:22.306
who's dying in that next room
over there.

12:22.374 --> 12:24.014
Oh, well, how 'bout we
concentrate

12:24.082 --> 12:25.623
on this family, Doctor?

12:25.690 --> 12:27.565
Well, the only other
option is your children.

12:27.633 --> 12:29.608
No, I won't put the kids
through that.

12:29.676 --> 12:31.685
Well, then I don't know
what else to do.

12:31.753 --> 12:33.258
You'll just have
to roll the dice

12:33.327 --> 12:34.833
and hope a kidney
comes through soon.

12:34.901 --> 12:38.015
Well... what about me?

12:38.083 --> 12:40.661
Is that possible, Doctor?

12:40.729 --> 12:42.805
Well... I suppose.

12:42.872 --> 12:44.413
Stranger things have
happened in medicine.

12:44.481 --> 12:46.356
I once tried
to clone a chicken.

12:46.424 --> 12:48.700
The result wound up
being a man-sized chicken

12:48.768 --> 12:51.313
that was incredibly hostile and
ended up escaping from the lab.

12:51.381 --> 12:53.624
Okay, Doc, we gotta have
a talk at some point,

12:53.692 --> 12:55.969
but Brian, are, are, you,
are you sure about this?

12:56.036 --> 12:58.849
Well, there's no harm in
seeing if I'm compatible.

13:03.037 --> 13:06.251
Well, according to these
results, Brian is a match.

13:06.302 --> 13:07.524
(all cheering)

13:07.592 --> 13:09.937
Hooray, yay, yeah,
ice cream sounds great.

13:10.004 --> 13:12.047
The only thing is...

13:12.114 --> 13:14.826
Mrs. Griffin, could I speak with
you and Brian alone, please?

13:14.894 --> 13:16.267
Well, of course.

13:16.318 --> 13:18.778
Kids, why don't you wait in the car?
Okay, Mom.

13:18.847 --> 13:20.956
Let's go find the big,
black sassy nurse.

13:21.007 --> 13:22.564
There's always one.

13:22.632 --> 13:25.411
NURSE: You can cry about it,
or you can decide to live.

13:25.480 --> 13:26.483
It's your choice.

13:26.551 --> 13:27.421
STEWIE:
Found her.

13:27.489 --> 13:29.330
Hey, w-w-what is it, Doc?

13:29.399 --> 13:32.311
Well, as a dog,
Brian's kidneys are smaller

13:32.379 --> 13:34.723
and don't have the capacity
of a human kidney.

13:34.791 --> 13:36.163
For the procedure
to work,

13:36.230 --> 13:38.943
we would need
to transplant two.

13:39.012 --> 13:41.657
But, but I only have two.

13:41.725 --> 13:42.930
That's right.

13:42.998 --> 13:44.638
The procedure would kill you.

13:46.447 --> 13:48.824
Ha-ha, that car's
gettin' towed!

13:53.166 --> 13:56.254
My God, Dr. Hartman,
isn't there any other way?

13:56.354 --> 13:58.731
I'm afraid not,
Mrs. Griffin.

13:58.799 --> 14:00.205
BRIAN:
I'll do it.

14:00.273 --> 14:01.947
What?

14:02.015 --> 14:04.058
I'll... I'll do it.

14:04.125 --> 14:05.698
I'll give you my kidneys.

14:05.767 --> 14:08.779
But Brian...
you'll die!

14:08.848 --> 14:11.325
Peter, you're my best friend.

14:11.394 --> 14:14.107
You, you gave me a home
when I didn't have one.

14:14.174 --> 14:16.719
And you've treated me like
a family member ever since.

14:16.787 --> 14:18.494
I wouldn't be here
if it wasn't for you.

14:18.561 --> 14:20.236
I'd probably be dead anyway,

14:20.303 --> 14:22.580
lying under
some freeway overpass.

14:22.648 --> 14:25.327
And I-I-I wanna,
I wanna return the favor.

14:25.395 --> 14:27.303
After all, I'm, I'm a dog.

14:27.371 --> 14:29.681
I have another eight years,
at best.

14:29.750 --> 14:34.337
I'm, I'm willing to give that up
so you can have another 40.

14:34.406 --> 14:36.011
Brian, I...

14:36.079 --> 14:37.452
I-I don't know what to say.

14:37.521 --> 14:39.964
You're savin' my life.

14:40.032 --> 14:41.639
Well, you saved mine.

14:41.707 --> 14:45.055
And, hey, we, we had a lotta
good years together, right?

14:45.123 --> 14:48.170
(voice breaking):
Yeah. We, uh...

14:48.238 --> 14:50.080
we sure did, buddy.

15:06.829 --> 15:08.318
Hello, everyone.

15:08.403 --> 15:10.044
Lois, did you put Stewie to bed?

15:10.112 --> 15:12.019
Yeah, he's asleep.

15:12.087 --> 15:14.364
Good. I'd rather he didn't know
about this till it's over.

15:14.432 --> 15:16.006
He's just a baby.

15:16.074 --> 15:20.325
Now, since the operation is
tomorrow, I thought it might be

15:20.394 --> 15:23.240
a good idea to just kinda close
the book, you know?

15:23.308 --> 15:26.254
So I just wanted to share
my final thoughts

15:26.323 --> 15:28.299
with each one of you.

15:28.366 --> 15:31.213
Meg, you're so beautiful
on the inside,

15:31.280 --> 15:33.657
but it's very important that
you know your limitations.

15:33.725 --> 15:34.930
What do you mean?

15:34.998 --> 15:36.874
Well, I know they say
"Reach for the stars,"

15:36.941 --> 15:38.213
but if we all did that,

15:38.281 --> 15:40.290
there'd be nobody left
here on Earth, right?

15:40.358 --> 15:41.462
I guess.

15:41.530 --> 15:43.169
Ha-ha, there you go.

15:43.238 --> 15:46.251
Chris, I have watched
you grow from a little boy

15:46.319 --> 15:48.128
into the young man
you are today.

15:48.195 --> 15:50.740
I know sometimes
life seems tough,

15:50.808 --> 15:53.285
but you just remember
to reach for the stars.

15:53.354 --> 15:55.095
You really think I can?

15:55.163 --> 15:57.021
I know you can,
slugger!

15:57.105 --> 15:59.951
And, Lois, I only ask this

16:00.019 --> 16:02.866
because of the dire situation
I'm in, but...

16:02.934 --> 16:06.918
can I, can I hump your leg
for 15 uninterrupted seconds?

16:06.986 --> 16:09.062
I suppose so, Brian.

16:12.714 --> 16:15.593
I-I said you could do it
for 15 seconds.

16:15.661 --> 16:16.733
Didn't need it.

16:21.959 --> 16:24.369
No! It can't be!

16:40.850 --> 16:42.826
Where... where am I?

16:42.893 --> 16:44.501
We're at the
playground, Brian.

16:44.569 --> 16:45.807
I kidnapped you.

16:45.875 --> 16:48.386
You and I are going
to spend the rest of our lives

16:48.454 --> 16:49.759
living right here
under the slide.

16:49.827 --> 16:51.668
Stewie, what the hell?
Are you outta your mind?

16:51.736 --> 16:53.309
I most certainly am not!

16:53.377 --> 16:55.621
I know what you're planning
to do, and I won't allow it!

16:55.688 --> 16:58.333
You're not going to kill
yourself for that fat bastard!

16:58.402 --> 16:59.908
Look, I've made up
my mind, all right?

16:59.976 --> 17:00.879
Now untie me.

17:00.948 --> 17:02.253
No, I will not!

17:02.321 --> 17:04.463
How dare you make plans
to abandon me!

17:04.532 --> 17:06.908
Come on, you, you can live
without a dog, Stewie,

17:06.976 --> 17:08.818
but you can't live
without a father.

17:08.886 --> 17:10.760
And besides, h-how exactly
do you think we're supposed

17:10.828 --> 17:12.167
to live here on
the playground?

17:12.235 --> 17:13.440
We'll do fine, Brian!

17:13.507 --> 17:15.148
Everything is right here
where we need it!

17:15.216 --> 17:17.058
The jungle gym is
the grocery store,

17:17.126 --> 17:18.365
the swing set is
the mall,

17:18.432 --> 17:20.039
the seesaw is
the post office,

17:20.107 --> 17:22.149
and the sandbox is
our summer home.

17:22.218 --> 17:23.490
Can you believe it,
Brian?

17:23.557 --> 17:25.030
We, we have
a summer home!

17:25.097 --> 17:27.273
I mean, yes, we'll,
we'll have to rent it out

17:27.342 --> 17:28.815
some years to help pay for...

17:28.883 --> 17:30.255
Oh, no, wait,
no, we won't,

17:30.322 --> 17:32.465
because I'm a famous race car
driver. I forgot!

17:32.534 --> 17:34.174
Stewie, you're not
a race car driver.

17:34.241 --> 17:36.350
And if you don't let
me go right now,

17:36.419 --> 17:37.657
you're gonna lose your dad.

17:37.725 --> 17:41.007
(sniffles) But Brian...

17:41.075 --> 17:43.587
I-I don't wanna lose anybody!

17:43.654 --> 17:48.376
I don't... I don't
wanna lose anybody!

17:48.444 --> 17:50.586
(sobbing)

17:50.654 --> 17:51.558
Oh, my God.

17:51.626 --> 17:53.065
Just stop, stop, just stop it.

17:53.133 --> 17:55.510
But Brian, I love you!

18:00.134 --> 18:01.472
Oh, God,
just wipe your nose, man!

18:04.153 --> 18:05.860
Can I have a hug,
Brian?!

18:05.928 --> 18:08.071
Oh, no, no, God,
no, no, not now, no!

18:08.138 --> 18:10.081
I want a hug!
I love...

18:10.148 --> 18:11.923
No, no. Just wipe...
Oh, God, there's no Kleenex.

18:11.991 --> 18:14.000
I love you, Brian!
I love you so much!

18:14.067 --> 18:15.507
Go... Go roll around
in the sandbox...

18:15.576 --> 18:16.848
in-in-in the summer house!

18:16.915 --> 18:18.121
Go... go to the summer house
and roll around!

18:25.410 --> 18:28.224
Well, here we go.
You know, buddy,

18:28.296 --> 18:30.238
I'm really gonna miss
havin' a dog.

18:30.306 --> 18:33.153
Hey, whaddaya say you be
my dog one last time, huh?

18:33.220 --> 18:34.660
Sure, Peter.
Whatever you want.

18:34.728 --> 18:36.234
Hey, hey, whassat?

18:36.302 --> 18:37.439
Whassat, boy?
Whassat?

18:37.507 --> 18:39.617
Uh, surgical equipment.

18:39.685 --> 18:41.258
Whose is that?
Whose is that?

18:41.326 --> 18:42.565
Probably belongs
to the hospital.

18:42.632 --> 18:44.540
Whassat for? Huh?
Whassat for?

18:44.609 --> 18:45.813
Um... cutting me open,

18:45.882 --> 18:47.355
harvesting my organs
and killing me.

18:47.423 --> 18:49.599
You gonna go get it?
You gonna get it, boy?

18:49.666 --> 18:51.709
No, I think, I think
the doctors need it.

18:51.776 --> 18:53.517
Can we, can we
just lay here?

18:55.193 --> 18:57.470
Dr. Hartman,
w-what're you...?

18:57.537 --> 18:59.245
You're not dressed
for surgery.

18:59.313 --> 19:00.451
I sure am.

19:00.519 --> 19:02.796
But I, I don't
understand.

19:02.864 --> 19:04.102
Well, you see, Mrs. Griffin,

19:04.170 --> 19:05.978
I found another donor
for your husband.

19:06.046 --> 19:08.055
You did?! Who?

19:08.123 --> 19:09.763
Me.
What?!

19:09.831 --> 19:11.639
It turns out
I'm a perfect match.

19:11.707 --> 19:14.251
And I'm willing to give up
one of my kidneys.

19:14.319 --> 19:15.927
Oh, my God,
Dr. Hartman,

19:15.994 --> 19:17.835
y-you'd do that
for us?!

19:17.903 --> 19:20.950
Well, the truth is you folks are
my only remaining patients.

19:21.018 --> 19:22.725
I couldn't afford
to lose two of you.

19:22.794 --> 19:24.468
What do you mean,
two of us?

19:24.536 --> 19:26.745
Well, chances are Peter never
would have survived the surgery.

19:26.813 --> 19:28.454
I mean, I mean,
dog kidneys?!

19:28.522 --> 19:30.731
I'm not even sure
dogs have kidneys.

19:30.799 --> 19:32.273
Do dogs have kidneys?

19:32.340 --> 19:33.545
Yes.

19:33.613 --> 19:36.090
Ah, this...
this is the guy.

19:36.158 --> 19:37.731
Oh, my God, Brian...

19:37.782 --> 19:38.753
(all clamoring)

19:38.839 --> 19:41.081
You're gonna live!

19:41.132 --> 19:43.090
Oh, I love you!
I love you!

19:48.685 --> 19:49.991
Good evening,
ladies and gentlemen.

19:50.059 --> 19:52.537
I'm happy to welcome you all
here to our auditorium

19:52.604 --> 19:55.082
for something that's
not a terrible play.

19:55.150 --> 19:57.092
In fact, tonight we're graced

19:57.160 --> 19:59.905
with the presence
of our Commander in Chief.

19:59.973 --> 20:03.691
But first, to introduce him
is a very talented young man,

20:03.758 --> 20:06.403
James Woods' own Chris Griffin.

20:06.471 --> 20:07.877
(applause)

20:07.946 --> 20:09.485
Thank you, Principal Shepherd.

20:09.553 --> 20:12.500
My sister Meg will be helping me
introduce the President,

20:12.568 --> 20:14.911
since we wrote this essay
together.

20:14.979 --> 20:18.998
"What is Hope?" by
Chris and Meg Griffin.

20:19.066 --> 20:21.510
Hope is what gets you
out of bed in the morning

20:21.577 --> 20:24.525
when it's the day of prom
and you haven't been asked.

20:24.592 --> 20:26.702
Hope pushes the
caterpillar

20:26.769 --> 20:29.984
through the cocoon and drives
the salmon upstream.

20:30.053 --> 20:33.301
Your breasts may be small
and your glasses may be thick,

20:33.368 --> 20:35.377
but hope doesn't
hold up a mirror.

20:35.445 --> 20:37.521
CHRIS:
Hope is a horizon we head for,

20:37.589 --> 20:40.234
leaving nothing behind us
but fear.

20:40.301 --> 20:42.277
And though we may never reach
our goals,

20:42.345 --> 20:46.029
it's hope that will save us
from who we once were.

20:54.169 --> 20:55.876
And now...

20:55.944 --> 20:57.752
Ladies and gentlemen...

20:57.820 --> 21:00.498
President Mike Obama!
President Barack Obama!

21:01.706 --> 21:03.413
(soft tremolo chord plays)

21:03.481 --> 21:07.566
♪ You gotta be sincere ♪

21:07.634 --> 21:09.141
(moans)

21:09.209 --> 21:14.097
♪ You gotta be sincere ♪

21:14.165 --> 21:16.643
♪ You gotta feel it here ♪

21:16.711 --> 21:19.323
♪ 'Cause if you feel it here ♪

21:19.391 --> 21:22.003
♪ Well, then you're gonna be ♪

21:22.071 --> 21:27.596
♪ Honestly sincere ♪

21:27.648 --> 21:28.652
(uptempo rhythm plays)

21:28.737 --> 21:30.159
♪ If you're really sincere ♪

21:30.244 --> 21:31.917
♪ If you're really
sincere ♪

21:31.969 --> 21:33.642
♪ If you feel it in here ♪

21:33.727 --> 21:35.468
♪ Then it's gotta be right! ♪

21:35.536 --> 21:36.908
♪ Oh, baby! ♪
(girls squealing)

21:36.975 --> 21:39.119
♪ Oh, honey! ♪

21:39.187 --> 21:40.627
♪ Hug me! ♪

21:40.694 --> 21:42.502
♪ Suffer! ♪

21:42.570 --> 21:43.539
(girls screaming)

21:43.540 --> 22:44.540
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru